# A local crate

A **local crate** is a package's crate in a directory on this machine: one
being written, or one checked out to be changed. This page follows one from
its first build to an installed package. The crate is `hello`, the worked
example of [[34-writing-an-extension|chapter 34]], copied beside a project:

```text
~/code/
├── hello/               the crate: Cargo.toml, src/, modules/
└── reading-club/        the project
    └── .hql/
        └── config.toml
```

Every command below runs in `~/code/reading-club`.

## Listing the crate

`[packages] paths` in the project's `.hql/config.toml` names the crate's
directory:

```toml
[packages]
paths = ["../hello"]
```

A path is read against the project root, the directory holding `.hql/`, so
`../hello` is `~/code/hello` from any working directory. A listed crate is a
package dependency of the project that is never installed, declared under
`dependencies` or pinned in `lock.toml`.

## The first run builds it

No command builds a listed crate. The first program run in the project
does, before the program is checked:

```sh
hql eval 'import hello
hello.greet("Ada")'
```

```text
hql: building package `hello` from /home/ada/code/hello
   Compiling hql-ext-hello v0.1.0 (/home/ada/code/hello)
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 1m 01s
"Hello, Ada!"
```

The build runs `cargo` in the crate, against the `hql` that is running, and
keeps its result under the crate's own `target/hql/hello/`: the library, a
copy of the module root and a `package.toml`, laid out as an installed
package is. The project's `.hql/` gains nothing. The first build compiles
the `hql` crate and its dependencies, which takes about a minute.

The second run finds the build current and starts no compiler:

```sh
hql eval 'import hello
hello.greet("Ada")'
```

```text
"Hello, Ada!"
```

## An edit rebuilds it

A build is current while it is newer than the crate's `Cargo.toml`, every
file under `src/` and every file under the module root. Changing one of
them makes the next run build again. Here the greeting in `src/lib.rs`
changes from `Hello` to `Good evening`:

```sh
hql eval 'import hello
hello.greet("Ada")'
```

```text
hql: building package `hello` from /home/ada/code/hello
   Compiling hql-ext-hello v0.1.0 (/home/ada/code/hello)
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.47s
"Good evening, Ada!"
```

Only the crate itself is compiled again. A change to a contract under
`modules/`, a new declaration or a changed signature, is picked up the same
way, and a contract the Rust no longer implements refuses the package with
the declaration's name.

A build that fails does not stop `hql` from starting. The package is
refused, and an `import` of one of its modules reports why:

```text
check error[unknown-name]: no module named `hello`: ...
```

## What the project sees

`hql doctor` reports the packages a program in the current directory runs
with, the listed crate among them:

```text
package  ok      hello 0.1.0 (hello)
```

`hql package list` lists installed packages only, and a listed crate is not
one, so it prints nothing here.

## Installing the crate

`install --path` copies the build into the project and records it:

```sh
hql package install --path ../hello
```

```text
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.12s
installed hello 0.1.0 (hello) in /home/ada/code/reading-club/.hql/packages/hello
```

```sh
hql package list
```

```text
hello 0.1.0 (hello) [project]
```

`.hql/config.toml` now declares the package beside the path:

```toml
[packages]
dependencies = ["hello"]
paths = ["../hello"]
```

and `.hql/lock.toml` pins it to the directory it was built from:

```toml
[[package]]
name = "hello"
version = "0.1.0"
source = "path"
path = "/home/ada/code/hello"
```

## Which one runs

With the crate both listed and installed, the listed crate is the one a
program reaches: a run discovers packages from `[packages] paths` first,
the project's `.hql/packages/` second and the HQL home's third, and the
same package found twice is the first found. Edits to the crate still take
effect on the next run.

Removing the `paths` entry leaves the installed copy, which no edit to the
crate changes:

```toml
[packages]
dependencies = ["hello"]
```

The installed package is the build made when it was installed. `hql package
rebuild hello` builds it again from the directory its pin names.

| The project has | A program runs | An edit to the crate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the crate under `paths` | the crate's own build | rebuilds on the next run |
| the crate under `paths`, and the package installed | the crate's own build | rebuilds on the next run |
| the package installed | the copy under `.hql/packages/` | has no effect until `hql package rebuild` |

A path pin names a directory on the machine that wrote it.
[A shared project](a-shared-project.hmd) is about what that means for a
project more than one person runs.
